cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this artifact, please cite it and the paper it accompanies once published. This artifact accompanies a prototype-stage manuscript that is not yet submitted (see README.md); cite with that context."
title: "DAIM-OS Self-Healing Artifact: Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Banjar
    given-names: Ameen
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0871-5153"
version: 0.2.0
date-released: 2026-08-13
license: Apache-2.0
# doi: TODO -- v0.1.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.21906993 (concept DOI
# 10.5281/zenodo.21906992, always resolves to latest); fill in the v0.2.0
# version-specific DOI once this tag's release is minted by Zenodo.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ameen-banjar/daim-os-self-healing-artifact"
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility artifact for "Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a
  DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
  (Paper 3 in the DAIM six-paper programme, prototype-stage, not yet
  submitted). Contains an event-driven repair agent (daim_link_agent.py)
  that subscribes to real OVSDB Interface.link_state notifications,
  recomputes an alternate path with breadth-first search over a declared
  topology graph, installs it through the existing DAIM-OS OVS adapter, and
  suppresses repeated repair processing on a flapping interface via an
  IDLE/ACTIVE/HELD-DOWN hold-down state machine that also reconciles an
  interface's true state once its hold-down window expires with no further
  event ever arriving for it (a stale-state defect found and fixed in
  v0.2.0 via external review); pure-logic unit tests for the path
  computation, the hold-down suppression behaviour, and the stale-state
  reconciliation fix, all runnable without Mininet/OVS; the network-level
  Stage 3 raw data (five autonomous link-failure repetitions, plus scripted
  non-autonomous switch/controller-failure conditions) and reports; and the
  figure-generation script and resulting figures used in the manuscript.
keywords:
  - software-defined networking
  - self-healing networks
  - event-driven fault detection
  - autonomous path recovery
  - OVSDB
  - OpenFlow
  - hold-down timers
  - control-plane resilience
  - reproducible systems research
